{"parentUuid":"d8cf3312-40d7-47a9-bca9-7137a331c9f5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6d5c0a1d-fdd5-4db0-8cbd-a5c82a0adcb7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Ge47Eo9dr1j3UxMXuEY61","type":"tool_result","content":"visible blades with all the scene's lights). Drawing the tufts near-to-far made no measurable\ndifference (reverted). If frame time matters, the levers are the patch radius (quality.blades)\nor a cheaper lighting model for the blades.\n\nDropped this round: rain occlusion under awnings (the near rain already fades within ~4 m, so\nnothing showed under the cafe awning).\n\nVerified this round: an enemy soldier close-up (AI frozen, camera placed by script) shows the\nskin and wool shading. Tried and dropped: lens dirt lit by the bloom (bloom.js) - it reads as a\nbigger mottled glow round the lights in this hazy scene, not as smudges; loses clarity.\n\nTried and dropped: reflections in window glass via the SSR pass (glassAlpha range in the scene\nalpha). The scene's alpha is not clean enough for a third range: the late/transparent draws\n(rain streaks, particles, grass edges) raise the alpha of ground pixels into the glass band,\nso wet ground got flagged as glass in streaks; the window panes themselves gave only a faint\nglint at grazing angles. Would need a separate G-buffer bit, not the alpha.\nTried and dropped: water running in the street's gutters (STREET in the ruts texture, a flat\nmirror strip along each cobble edge with downstream wavelets). Placed right, but under this\nlighting a flat dark stream reflecting dark house fronts is indistinguishable from the wet\ncobbles beside it in A/B captures; not worth the shader cost and complexity.\nTried and left out (a taste call for the user): a bleach-bypass grade in post.js (desaturate the\nmid tones, contrast about mid grey). At 0.35 the street gets a silvery Saving-Private-Ryan look,\nbut the night frame sits mostly below mid grey, so the meadow and forest went murky; a lower\npivot would be needed. Left the grade as it was.\nChecked: low and medium quality presets compile and render all the new effects through their\nfallbacks (no late pass: rain/flames/plumes/fireballs with the scene; window light and rain sheets\noff); no shader errors.\n\nTried and shelved: eye adaptation in the grade pass (a 1x1 half-float ping-pong stalls: the\nper-frame step is under half-float resolution; and the flare-lit meadow measures inside the\nnormal night range, so it would not have tamed that glare). WIP copy in the session scratchpad.\n\nObserved, not fixed (pre-existing, invisible): adding or removing any swapping pass in the effects\nchain changes ~150 pixels by 3-7 levels along a hairline of slope 0.75 through (256,0) at\n1024x768 - some pass's rounding depends on buffer parity. Neither parity shows a visible seam.\n\nTried and dropped: raindrops beaded on rifles/outfits (unreadable at this resolution and lighting).\n\nTesting tip: a headless Chrome (playwright's chromium_headless_shell) renders on the real GPU\nwithout the browser pane; driven over CDP from a scratch script (needs the sandbox off to start).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6162cf29-4790-451c-b18b-1001cf5d9561","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:14:29.998Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"visible blades with all the scene's lights). Drawing the tufts near-to-far made no measurable\ndifference (reverted). If frame time matters, the levers are the patch radius (quality.blades)\nor a cheaper lighting model for the blades.\n\nDropped this round: rain occlusion under awnings (the near rain already fades within ~4 m, so\nnothing showed under the cafe awning).\n\nVerified this round: an enemy soldier close-up (AI frozen, camera placed by script) shows the\nskin and wool shading. Tried and dropped: lens dirt lit by the bloom (bloom.js) - it reads as a\nbigger mottled glow round the lights in this hazy scene, not as smudges; loses clarity.\n\nTried and dropped: reflections in window glass via the S